### Describe the bug

Since 6.6.6 every **hit** on the result cache (`findOne`/`find` with the `cache` option) re-hydrates *populated relations* in a dirty state. The very next `em.flush()` then issues a spurious `UPDATE` of that relation's `json`/`jsonb` columns, even though nothing was touched. A cache **miss** is fine, and a cached **root** entity is fine — only the nested, populated entities are affected.

Bisect:

| version | result |
| --- | --- |
| 6.4.13 | ok |
| 6.6.5 | ok |
| **6.6.6** | **spurious UPDATE** |
| 6.6.16 | spurious UPDATE |

First bad commit is b0ed700647ef31ddb1e0adb5a844f1c19093a6c9 ("fix result caching for custom types", closes #7112).

**Mechanism**

`EntityManager.tryCache` re-creates the cached result with `recomputeSnapshot: true`:

```js
// packages/core/src/EntityManager.ts
const createOptions = {
  merge: true,
  convertCustomTypes: false,
  refresh,
  recomputeSnapshot: true,
};
```

`EntityFactory.create` honours that flag only for the entity it was called with:

```js
// packages/core/src/entity/EntityFactory.ts
if (options.recomputeSnapshot) {
  wrapped.__originalEntityData = this.comparator.prepareEntity(entity);
}
```

but the compiled hydrator creates the populated relations through a nested `factory.create(...)` whose options do **not** carry `recomputeSnapshot`:

```js
// packages/core/src/hydration/ObjectHydrator.ts
entity${entityKey} = factory.${method}('${prop.type}', data${dataKey},
  { initialized: true, merge: true, newEntity, convertCustomTypes, normalizeAccessors, schema });
```

So for the nested entity `__originalEntityData` stays the raw cached POJO (`payload` as an object), while `Comparator.prepareEntity` later produces the DB form (`payload` as a JSON string). The comparator sees a difference, the UoW computes a change set, and the flush writes the row back.

Change set observed on the cache hit:

```
update Child {"payload":"{\"a\":1,\"b\":{\"c\":2}}"}
```

Production impact: with a warm cache and many concurrent workers this rewrites the same rows over and over on every request, which on PostgreSQL produced a deadlock storm.

Possible direction (not a proposal, just where it seems to sit): the nested `factory.create` calls in the hydrator should propagate `recomputeSnapshot`, or `tryCache` should recompute the snapshot for the whole loaded graph rather than only the root.

### Reproduction

Minimal, single file, in-memory sqlite. `npm i @mikro-orm/core@6.6.16 @mikro-orm/sqlite@6.6.16 tsx` and `npx tsx repro.ts` — exits 1 on the bug, 0 on 6.6.5.

```ts
import { Entity, MikroORM, OneToOne, PrimaryKey, Property, Ref } from '@mikro-orm/core';
import { SqliteDriver } from '@mikro-orm/sqlite';

@Entity()
class Child {

  @PrimaryKey({ type: 'number' })
  id!: number;

  @Property({ type: 'string' })
  name!: string;

  @Property({ type: 'json' })
  payload!: Record<string, unknown>;

}

@Entity()
class Parent {

  @PrimaryKey({ type: 'number' })
  id!: number;

  @Property({ type: 'string' })
  name!: string;

  @OneToOne(() => Child, { ref: true })
  child!: Ref<Child>;

}

const queries: string[] = [];

const orm = await MikroORM.init({
  entities: [Parent, Child],
  dbName: ':memory:',
  driver: SqliteDriver,
  debug: ['query'],
  allowGlobalContext: true,
  colors: false,
  logger: msg => queries.push(msg),
});

await orm.schema.createSchema();

const child = orm.em.create(Child, { id: 1, name: 'child', payload: { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } } });
orm.em.create(Parent, { id: 1, name: 'parent', child });
await orm.em.flush();
orm.em.clear();

// ---- fork A: cache MISS, warms the result cache -------------------------
const emA = orm.em.fork();
queries.length = 0;
await emA.findOne(Parent, 1, { populate: ['child'], cache: 1000 });
const missQueries = [...queries];

// ---- fork B: cache HIT --------------------------------------------------
const emB = orm.em.fork();
queries.length = 0;
await emB.findOne(Parent, 1, { populate: ['child'], cache: 1000 });
const hitQueries = [...queries];

// nothing was modified, this flush must be a no-op
emB.getUnitOfWork().computeChangeSets();
const changeSets = emB.getUnitOfWork().getChangeSets().map(cs => `${cs.type} ${cs.name} ${JSON.stringify(cs.payload)}`);
queries.length = 0;
await emB.flush();
const flushQueries = [...queries];


console.log('--- queries on cache MISS (fork A) ---');
missQueries.forEach(q => console.log(q));
console.log('\n--- queries on cache HIT (fork B) ---');
console.log(hitQueries.length === 0 ? '(none - served from cache)' : hitQueries.join('\n'));
console.log('\n--- queries on flush() after the cache HIT ---');
console.log(flushQueries.length === 0 ? '(none)' : flushQueries.join('\n'));

const isSelect = (q: string) => /\[query] select /i.test(q);
const isUpdate = (q: string) => /\[query] update /i.test(q);

let failed = false;

if (missQueries.some(isSelect)) {
  console.log('\n[ok] cache MISS issued a SELECT, as expected');
} else {
  console.log('\n[FAIL] expected a SELECT on cache miss');
  failed = true;
}

if (hitQueries.some(isSelect)) {
  console.log('[FAIL] expected the second findOne to be served from the result cache, but a SELECT was issued');
  failed = true;
} else {
  console.log('[ok] second findOne was served from the result cache (no SELECT)');
}

console.log(`\nchange sets computed after the cache HIT: ${changeSets.length}`);
changeSets.forEach(cs => console.log('   ' + cs));

if (flushQueries.some(isUpdate)) {
  console.log('[FAIL] flush() after a cache HIT issued a spurious UPDATE of the populated relation:');
  flushQueries.filter(isUpdate).forEach(q => console.log('   ' + q));
  failed = true;
} else {
  console.log('[ok] flush() after a cache HIT issued no UPDATE');
}

await orm.close(true);

console.log(failed ? '\nRESULT: FAILED (bug reproduced)' : '\nRESULT: PASSED (no spurious update)');
process.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);
```

Output on **6.6.16** (`@mikro-orm/sqlite`):

```
--- queries on cache MISS (fork A) ---
[query] select `p0`.*, `c1`.`id` as `c1__id`, `c1`.`name` as `c1__name`, `c1`.`payload` as `c1__payload` from `parent` as `p0` inner join `child` as `c1` on `p0`.`child_id` = `c1`.`id` where `p0`.`id` = ? limit ? [took 0 ms, 1 result]

--- queries on cache HIT (fork B) ---
(none - served from cache)

--- queries on flush() after the cache HIT ---
[query] begin
[query] update `child` set `payload` = ? where `id` = ? [took 0 ms, 1 row affected]
[query] commit

[ok] cache MISS issued a SELECT, as expected
[ok] second findOne was served from the result cache (no SELECT)

change sets computed after the cache HIT: 1
   update Child {"payload":"{\"a\":1,\"b\":{\"c\":2}}"}
[FAIL] flush() after a cache HIT issued a spurious UPDATE of the populated relation:
   [query] update `child` set `payload` = ? where `id` = ? [took 0 ms, 1 row affected]

RESULT: FAILED (bug reproduced)
```

Output on **6.6.5**, same file:

```
--- queries on cache MISS (fork A) ---
[query] select `p0`.*, `c1`.`id` as `c1__id`, `c1`.`name` as `c1__name`, `c1`.`payload` as `c1__payload` from `parent` as `p0` inner join `child` as `c1` on `p0`.`child_id` = `c1`.`id` where `p0`.`id` = ? limit ? [took 1 ms, 1 result]

--- queries on cache HIT (fork B) ---
(none - served from cache)

--- queries on flush() after the cache HIT ---
(none)

[ok] cache MISS issued a SELECT, as expected
[ok] second findOne was served from the result cache (no SELECT)

change sets computed after the cache HIT: 0
[ok] flush() after a cache HIT issued no UPDATE

RESULT: PASSED (no spurious update)
```

Same result on PostgreSQL 17 with `@mikro-orm/postgresql@6.6.16` and `@Property({ type: 'jsonb' })`:

```
--- queries on cache HIT (fork B) ---
(none - served from cache)

--- queries on flush() after the cache HIT ---
[query] begin
[query] update "child" set "payload" = $1 where "id" = $2 [took 0 ms, 1 row affected]
[query] commit
```

### Expected behavior

A `flush()` right after a result-cache hit, with nothing modified, issues no queries — the same as on a cache miss and the same as on 6.6.5.

