### Describe the bug

`em.populate(entities, ['collection.relation'], { where })` derives the condition for the nested
relation by keeping only the branches of a `$or` that name it, then re-wrapping the survivors in
`$or`. A two-branch disjunction whose other branch is silent about that relation becomes a
one-branch `$or`, which is a requirement rather than an alternative. The condition applied below
the first level is therefore stricter than the one the caller wrote.

That extraction is the implementation of `PopulateHint.INFER`, and it is correct when the caller
asks for it. The problem is that `em.populate()` asks for it unconditionally: `findChildren()` does
`populateWhere ??= 'infer'` while `Configuration.ts` sets `populateWhere: PopulateHint.ALL` as the
ORM-wide default. `em.find()` resolves the same option through
`options.populateWhere ?? this.config.get('populateWhere')` and behaves as documented;
`em.populate()` does not, and there is no way to reach `ALL` semantics through it short of passing
the flag by hand on every call.

The corruption shows up in two shapes depending on strategy.

**`select-in`**: the collection query is correct and both rows come back, but the nested query
carries the extracted single branch, so the target of the row that only matched the *other* branch
is never fetched and its required many-to-one is left null.

```sql
-- collection query, correct
select `b0`.* from `book` as `b0`
  inner join `publisher` as `p1` on `b0`.`publisher_id` = `p1`.`id`
  where `b0`.`author_id` in (1, 2, 3)
    and (`p1`.`open` = true or `b0`.`author_id` in (2))   -- 2 results

-- nested query, one branch of the `$or` applied on its own
select `p0`.* from `publisher` as `p0`
  where `p0`.`id` in (11, 10) and `p0`.`open` = true      -- 1 result
```

Book 101 keeps its row but ends up with `publisher === undefined`, on a property declared
non-nullable.

**`balanced` (the default) and `joined`**: the branch lands in the join's `ON` clause instead, where
it filters every row, so the row never reaches its parent's collection at all.

```sql
select `b0`.*, `p1`.`id` as `p1__id`, `p1`.`open` as `p1__open` from `book` as `b0`
  inner join `publisher` as `p1`
    on `b0`.`publisher_id` = `p1`.`id` and `p1`.`open` = true   -- <-- the extracted branch
  where `b0`.`author_id` in (1, 2, 3)
    and (`p1`.`open` = true or `b0`.`author_id` in (2))         -- 1 result
```

The `where` clause still holds the full disjunction, so the intent is visible right next to the
`ON` clause that overrides it. `QueryBuilder.mergeOnConditions` carries the same decomposition into
the join condition, which is what turns the inner join into a row filter here.

#### Why I have not attached a fix

Making `findChildren()` honor the configured default is a one-line change. It makes both new tests
pass and leaves `tests/issues/GH1882.test.ts` green, since that test requests
`populateWhere: PopulateHint.INFER` explicitly on `em.find` and is unaffected by what `em.populate`
defaults to.

It breaks `tests/features/serialization/GH6658.test.ts`. That test does

```ts
await orm.em.populate(users, ['shop.orders'], {
  where: { shop: { orders: { category: 'A' } } },
});
```

and the nested `orders` filter reaches the nested query only because the resolved INFER
populate-where object is what gets extracted and passed down. Turn INFER off and `options.where`
alone does not propagate below the first level, so the filter silently vanishes.

So the accidental INFER is load-bearing for nested `where` propagation in `em.populate`, and the two
behaviors are currently the same code path. Separating them needs a propagation path for a nested
`where` that does not go through the populate-hint machinery, plus a decision on what
`em.populate(..., { where })` is supposed to mean below the first level. That call seemed better
made by a maintainer than guessed at, so this is a test-only report. Four approaches were tried;
each broke GH1882, GH6658, or the new tests. Happy to open a PR if the direction gets settled.

#### Related defect in the same code path

`EntityLoader.ts:710`:

```ts
if ([ReferenceKind.ONE_TO_ONE, ReferenceKind.MANY_TO_ONE].includes(prop.kind) && items.length !== children.length) {
```

This block reads "fewer children came back than there were items" as "the target row is missing" and
writes `null` into both the property and `__originalEntityData`. A populate condition that filtered
the target out is not a missing row, and nulling a non-nullable reference because of it is data
corruption regardless of how the condition got there. It is the mechanism behind the `select-in`
shape above, and it would misfire the same way for any filtered populate, so it seems worth looking
at alongside the extraction rather than after it.

#### Prior report of the same shape

#2616 describes the same disjunction problem against `em.find` on v4, when `INFER` was still the
default everywhere.

