### Describe the bug

In a multi-schema Postgres setup, a nested collection filter (`$some` inside another `$some`) that crosses from an entity in one schema into entities in a different schema generates SQL that qualifies the nested table with the **root** entity's schema instead of the target entity's own `@Entity({ schema })`.

The first-level `$some` join is qualified correctly. Only the nested (second-level) subquery inherits the wrong schema, so Postgres throws `relation "<wrong_schema>.<table>" does not exist`.

This worked in v6 and broke in 7.1.4. A single-level `$some` works; only the nested case is affected.

### Reproduction

Three entities across two schemas — `Author` in schema `a`; `Book` and `Review` in schema `b`:

  ```ts
  @Entity({ schema: 'a' })
  class Author {
    @PrimaryKey() id!: number;

    @ManyToMany({ entity: () => Book, owner: true })
    books = new Collection<Book>(this);
  }

  @Entity({ schema: 'b' })
  class Book {
    @PrimaryKey() id!: number;

    @OneToMany(() => Review, (r) => r.book)
    reviews = new Collection<Review>(this);
  }

  class Review {
    @PrimaryKey() id!: number;

    @ManyToOne(() => Book) book!: Book;

    @Property() rating!: number;
  }
  ```

  Query with a nested `$some` (schema `a` → schema `b`):

  ```ts
  await em.find(Author, {
    books: { $some: { reviews: { $some: { rating: 5 } } } },
  });
  ```

  Generated SQL on 7.1.4 — the innermost subquery selects `Book` as `"a"."book"` (the root `Author` schema) instead of `"b"."book"`:

  ```sql
  select "a0".* from "a"."author" as "a0"
  where "a0"."id" in (
    select "a0"."id" from "a"."author" as "a0"
    inner join "a"."author_books" as "a2" on "a0"."id" = "a2"."author_id"
    inner join "b"."book" as "b1" on "a2"."book_id" = "b1"."id"     -- correct schema
    where "b1"."id" in (
      select "a0"."id" from "a"."book" as "a0"                       -- WRONG: should be "b"."book"
      inner join "b"."review" as "b1" on "a0"."id" = "b1"."book_id"
      where "b1"."rating" = 5
    )
  );
  ```

  Expected — the nested subquery should resolve `Book`'s own schema, matching the first-level join:

  ```sql
    where "b1"."id" in (
      select "a0"."id" from "b"."book" as "a0"
      inner join "b"."review" as "b1" on "a0"."id" = "b1"."book_id"
      where "b1"."rating" = 5
    )
  ```

  Resulting error:

  ```
  TableNotFoundException: relation "a.book" does not exist
  ```

